    ('homelessness-response', 3, 'Brooks''s documented approach pairs reasonable public-space rules with connecting people to housing and services — consistent with her professional background as a Licensed Clinical Social Worker with a master''s degree in social work. She voted with the unanimous council on June 26, 2023 for the time/place/manner camping ordinance that banned camping in most public places while designating a legal overnight location next to the police station and tying enforcement to Washington County''s shelter-bed availability tracking. At the December 9, 2024 council meeting, as the Plambeck Gardens affordable units opened, she inquired about prioritizing Tualatin residents for housing and services, pushing staff on how locally unhoused and rent-burdened residents would actually be connected to the new resources. That is the invest-in-outreach-shelter-and-services-while-enforcing-reasonable-public-space-rules chair, not enforcement-first minimalism and not housing-first with no enforcement.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/new-ordinance-limits-hours-and-locations-for-houseless-camping/', 'https://mccmeetings.blob.core.usgovcloudapi.net/tualtnor-pubu/MEET-Packet-15ec587d6bfe4dc8ae561fc58af200fb.pdf', 'https://www.tualatinoregon.gov/administration/camping-regulations']::text[]),
    ('climate-change', 2, 'Brooks is the council''s most consistently documented climate advocate and treats decarbonization as urgent rather than gradual. In September 2022 she was one of three dissenters when the council voted 4-3 to join 13 cities suing Oregon to halt the Climate-Friendly and Equitable Communities rules, saying "I''m not interested in joining a lawsuit. I think the (costs) are going to get multiplied. I think the relationships are going to get strained," and instead declaring "I''m very interested in creative solutions" and that she wanted to "be a change maker on this as an elected official, to represent my constituents that care deeply about this climate crisis." In her August and September 2022 Council Corner columns championing the Climate Action Plan, she warned Tualatin''s days over 90 degrees could rise from 6 to nearly 60 per summer and argued that "by switching to carbon-free electricity and fuels and employing strategies such as carbon sequestration to draw down the greenhouse gasses in the atmosphere, we can avoid the worst effects of climate change." At the April 23, 2025 State of the City she presented the CAP''s net-zero-by-2050 goal and concrete measures (energy capture from water pipes, tree inventory, habitat improvement). Her "climate crisis" framing, advocacy for switching to carbon-free electricity and fuels, and defense of binding state climate mandates against her own city''s litigation best match the rapid-transition chair rather than the gradualist or market-driven chairs.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/tualatin-among-13-cities-suing-oregon-to-halt-new-climate-regulations/', 'https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/council-corner-september-2022/', 'https://tualatinlife.com/featured/state-of-the-city-tualatin-is-going-full-steam-ahead/']::text[]),
